  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Given that $c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $c.$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,c\\,| & =\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $c$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}c \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
